---
title: asRegion
summary: Mark any imported component as a region, whether it is a named import, a default import or a barrel re-export. It covers the imports the `with { … }` attribute form cannot reach, and works the same in .svelte, .ts and .js.
related:
  - regions/held-regions
  - macros/regions
  - macros/overview
---

The usual way to mark a region is with an import attribute:

```svelte
import Counter from './Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
```

An import attribute only works on a **default import of one file**, though. Sometimes the only way to reach a component is through a **barrel**, such as a design system's `index.ts` or a `@company/ui` package, where it arrives as a **named** import and there is nowhere to put the `with { … }`:

```ts
import { Header, Footer } from '@company/ui'; // named — no `with { … }` possible
```

`import.meta.og.asRegion(Comp, options)` handles that case by marking **any** imported component (named, default, or from a barrel) as a region:

```svelte
<script>
  import { Header } from '@company/ui';
  const HeaderIsland = import.meta.og.asRegion(Header, { wake: 'load' });
</script>

<HeaderIsland {...props} />
```

## The options are exactly `with { … }`

`options` is an object that accepts **exactly** what an import attribute accepts, no more and no less, because both go through the same parser and so cannot drift apart. Every mode works:

```svelte
const HeaderIsland = import.meta.og.asRegion(Header, { wake: 'load' });                     // placed island
const ChartHole    = import.meta.og.asRegion(Chart, { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'visible' }); // server hole
const BlockRaw     = import.meta.og.asRegion(Block, { region: 'raw' });                       // held → region(BlockRaw, …)
```

The output is also identical to the `with { … }` form, except that the generated entry imports the component by its **export name**, so region identity is keyed on `source#exportName`. Two named exports from one barrel are therefore two distinct regions and never collide.

## Tree-shaking is preserved

A region taken from a large mixed barrel ships **only its own component**, not the whole barrel. The generated entry does `import { Header } from '@company/ui'`, which the bundler tree-shakes exactly as it would anywhere else, so a 100-component barrel with one region marked pulls in one component.

## Works in `.svelte`, `.ts`, and `.js`

The same call produces one of two things, depending on where it lives:

- In a **`.svelte`** host it creates a **placed island**, which you render inline (`<HeaderIsland/>`, or `<svelte:component this={HeaderIsland}/>`).
- In a **`.ts` / `.js`** registry or remote it creates a [**held binding**](/docs/regions/held-regions), a mountable value that a third-party renderer can place or that `region()` can turn into a held region:

```ts title=registry.ts
import { Header } from '@company/ui';

// a mountable held binding — Builder.io's <svelte:component> places it directly,
// and region() can still cross it over the wire
const HeaderRegion = import.meta.og.asRegion(Header, { wake: 'visible' });

export const registry = [{ name: 'header', component: HeaderRegion }];
```

This one line is what turns a component imported from a barrel into something a CMS or page builder can render. [Mountable `.ts` bindings](/docs/regions/held-regions#mountable-ts-bindings) describes what such a binding can do.

## The rules

- **Top-level `const` only.** Write `const Local = import.meta.og.asRegion(Comp, options)`, one binding per statement, at the top level of the module or `<script>`. It compiles to a hoisted import, so it can never appear inside a loop, function, block, larger expression, `<script module>` or markup, and each of those is a build error.
- **The first argument is a component you imported**: a bare identifier bound to a top-level import (named or default, but not `import * as`).
- **`options` is an object literal.** There is no string shorthand; write a `{ wake: 'load' }` object, as you would in a `with { … }` clause.
- **One mechanism per component.** A component already marked with an import attribute cannot also be passed to `asRegion`, so pick one.

Misuse fails the build with an `[ogygia]` message that names the file and the fix. `asRegion` is a compile-time construct that is rewritten to a hoisted binding import, so it does not exist at runtime.
